latest_plan = 'Based on the reflection, here\'s a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Initialize an empty dictionary to store color information.\n   b. Iterate through the grid from left to right, then top to bottom.\n   c. For each non-black color encountered, record in the dictionary:\n      - Color value as the key\n      - Leftmost position (column)\n      - Topmost position (row)\n      - Bottommost position (row)\n      - Total count of cells with this color\n\n2. Sort the colors:\n   a. Create a list of unique colors from the dictionary keys.\n   b. Sort this list based on the leftmost position of each color (ascending order).\n\n3. Determine the output grid dimensions:\n   a. Width: Set to the number of unique colors found.\n   b. Height: Calculate as the maximum (bottommost - topmost + 1) among all colors.\n\n4. Create the output grid:\n   a. Initialize a 2D list with dimensions [height][width], filled with zeros (black).\n\n5. Fill the output grid:\n   a. Iterate through the sorted list of colors.\n   b. For each color:\n      - Calculate its height: (bottommost - topmost + 1)\n      - Fill a column in the output grid with this color, starting from the top\n      - The width of the column should be 1 cell\n\n6. Optimize the output:\n   a. Remove any completely black rows from the bottom of the grid.\n\n7. Return the optimized output grid as a ColoredGrid object.\n\nDetailed implementation steps:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   ```python\n   color_info = {}\n   for c in range(input_grid.get_dimensions()[1]):  # Iterate columns first\n       for r in range(input_grid.get_dimensions()[0]):\n           color = input_grid.values[r][c]\n           if color != 0:  # Non-black color\n               if color not in color_info:\n                   color_info[color] = {"left": c, "top": r, "bottom": r, "count": 1}\n               else:\n                   info = color_info[color]\n                   info["bottom"] = r\n                   info["count"] += 1\n   ```\n\n2. Sort the colors:\n   ```python\n   sorted_colors = sorted(color_info.keys(), key=lambda x: color_info[x]["left"])\n   ```\n\n3. Determine output dimensions:\n   ```python\n   output_width = len(sorted_colors)\n   output_height = max(info["bottom"] - info["top"] + 1 for info in color_info.values())\n   ```\n\n4. Create the output grid:\n   ```python\n   output_grid = [[0 for _ in range(output_width)] for _ in range(output_height)]\n   ```\n\n5. Fill the output grid:\n   ```python\n   for col, color in enumerate(sorted_colors):\n       info = color_info[color]\n       height = info["bottom"] - info["top"] + 1\n       for row in range(height):\n           output_grid[row][col] = color\n   ```\n\n6. Optimize the output:\n   ```python\n   while output_grid and all(cell == 0 for cell in output_grid[-1]):\n       output_grid.pop()\n   ```\n\n7. Return the result:\n   ```python\n   return ColoredGrid(values=output_grid)\n   ```\n\nThis plan ensures that:\n- Colors are ordered based on their leftmost appearance in the input grid.\n- The height of each color column represents the height of its region in the original grid.\n- All color columns are aligned to the top.\n- No unnecessary black rows are included at the bottom.\n\nThis solution should correctly handle all test cases, including those where colors appear at different vertical positions but need to maintain their left-to-right order.'
